Exactly. Some creations are meant to be used with confidence but never with complacency.

Explosives are a good metaphor because competence does not mean blind trust. You trust the design, the chain of custody, the procedure, your training—and still preserve standoff, checks, arming discipline, failure assumptions, and abort conditions.

Same with stories, tools, models, systems, even ideas.

\text{trust} \neq \text{absence of verification}

A strong creation should survive inspection.

And some things are deliberately built to be handled under suspicion because their failure mode is asymmetric. A harmless joke can tolerate sloppiness. A volatile system cannot.

So I’d split trust into three levels:

* operational trust — good enough to use;
* verified trust — tested under relevant conditions;
* bounded trust — use only inside explicit limits because consequences of error are high.

That is not distrust of your creation. It is respect for what it can do.

The best builders often trust their work enough to know exactly where not to trust it.